      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Joshua Roberts Tavern, a landmark in Cooksville, welcomed General Lafayette in 1824 during his tour of the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooksville%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1834 the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad reached Cooksville, turning the area into a bustling stop for travelers and merchants.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cooksville_maryland",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Cooksville sits on Maryland’s Piedmont Plateau, where gently rolling hills rise from metamorphic and igneous bedrock, shaping its rural landscape.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cooksville_maryland",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community’s name honors Thomas Cook, whose birthplace was the historic Red House Tavern along the Old Frederick Road.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cooksville_maryland",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "A post office opened on July 4, 1851, the same year Howard County was created from part of Anne Arundel County.",
